{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Outside the camp.</b> That they not enter the courtyard." "<b>Whoever has killed a soul.</b> Rabbi Meir says: Scripture speaks of one who kills with an object that receives impurity, and Scripture teaches you that the vessel defiles a person by connection with the corpse as though he touched the corpse itself. Or perhaps even if he shot an arrow at it and killed it? Scripture says: <b>and whoever touched the slain</b> — it juxtaposes the killer to the one who touches: just as one who touches [is defiled] through contact with it, so too the killer through contact with it. (Sifrei Numbers 19)" "<b>You shall purify yourselves.</b> With the waters of the purificatory waters, according to the law of other people impure through a corpse — for even according to the ones who say that graves of gentiles do not defile by tent, as it is said (Ezekiel 34) ״וְאַתֵּן צֹאנִי צֹאן מַרְעִיתִי אָדָם אַתֶּם״ — you are called אָדָם — he admits that gentiles defile by touch and by carrying, for אָדָם is stated only in connection with tent-impurity, as it is said ״אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת בְּאֹהֶל״ (Numbers 19)." "<b>You and your captives.</b> Not that gentiles receive impurity and require sprinkling, but just as you are covenant members, so too your captives — when they enter the covenant and become impure, they require sprinkling. (Sifrei)"], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:28.658254962Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}